    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 071735\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1035 AM PDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Well above-normal temperatures will persist through Friday, with \n  dangerous heat expected in Death Valley.\n\n* Chances for gusty winds, cooler temperatures, and potentially\n  widespread precipitation arrive Sunday through Tuesday. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through early next week.\n\nWeak shortwave that brought a few showers and thunderstorms to \ncentral Nevada Tuesday will continue to shift east today as high \npressure strengthen over the region. There will still be enough\nmid-level moisture over the area to bring a few afternoon clouds,\nbut no precipitation is expected. Temperatures will peak this \nafternoon through Friday with readings generally 10-15 degrees \nabove normal. Major to extreme heat risk will persist in Death \nValley as daytime high temperatures climb into the 100s each day, \nand may approach 110 degrees during peak heating. \n\nSome big changes could be heading our way this weekend and early \nnext week. First, a deep upper level trough is forecast to dig \nsouthward out of the Pacific Northwest, and interact with a weak \nupper low off of the southern California. This weak low is \nexpected to absorb remnant moisture from Hurricane Rachel and \neventually get pulled northeast into southern California and \nArizona Sunday as the trough continues to dig. The models are \nindicating fairly significant moisture getting pulled into the \narea with PWs forecast to be around 200-250 percent of normal for \nthis time of year. There has been very little overall change with \nthe timing and track, but the latest model runs have trended a \nlittle further west which could put areas like eastern San \nBernardino, Clark, Lincoln, and Mohave counties with the potential\nof seeing widespread areas of heavy rain Sunday afternoon through\nMonday. Precipitation chances look to hang on at least through \nmid-week as a closed low develops over central Nevada and is slow \nto exit. Although the models remain in good agreement, we could \nstill see changes the the depth of the trough and the track of \nHurricane Rachel which would alter the overall forecast. Outside \nof the precipitation chances, temperatures trending below normal \nalong with gusty conditions can also be expected. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Winds \nfollow typical daily directional patterns with speeds under 10 knots \nthrough the forecast period. FEW mid level clouds develop today and \ntomorrow during the afternoon with bases at or above 12kft.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Winds follow typical \ndaily directional patterns with speeds of 10 knots or less for most \nlocations. The exception will be KIFP, where breezy northerly winds \nincluding gusts to around 20 knots pick up today and tomorrow in the \nafternoon. VFR conditions are expected areawide with cloud bases at \nor above 10kft. \n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE...Numerous climate locations have a forecast high\ntemperature and/or forecast warm low temperature within 3 degrees of \nthe daily record.\n\nThe table below shows the daily record maximum temperature and the \nyear the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which records \nare in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nMAX           WED, OCT 7  THU, OCT 8  FRI, OCT 9\n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     99(1978)*   98(2024)*   97(2024)*\nBishop        94(1965)*   95(1996)    93(2015)\nNeedles       107(2024)   107(2024)   104(1996)*\nDaggett       102(2024)   101(1996)*  103(1980)\nKingman       99(2024)    98(1996)    98(1910)\nDesert Rock   99(2024)    98(1996)*   97(1996)\nDeath Valley  109(2024)*  112(1996)*  112(1996)\n\nThe table below shows the daily record warm minimum temperature and \nthe year the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which \nrecords are in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nWARM MIN      WED, OCT 7  THU, OCT 8  FRI, OCT 9\n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     75(2024)*   76(2024)*   72(2024)*\nBishop        57(1943)    51(1960)    61(2025)\nNeedles       76(1947)*   76(2024)*   76(1988)*\nDaggett       72(2024)*   69(1980)*   69(1996)*\nKingman       69(1947)*   67(1960)*   68(1926)*\nDesert Rock   65(1980)*   70(2024)    65(1991)*\nDeath Valley  80(1943)*   85(1964)*   78(2024)*\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Gorelow\nAVIATION...Meltzer\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
